    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantum entanglement is when two tiny particles get linked together in a special way, so they behave like a single system even when separated by huge distances. The weird part is that when you measure one particle (say, checking its spin), you instantly know what the other particle's outcome will be — no matter how far apart they are. Einstein famously called it \"spooky action at a distance\" because it seemed to violate our everyday intuition about how the universe works. Importantly, this doesn't let you send information faster than light, but it does show that entangled particles share a deeper connection than anything in classical physics.",
